// Histogram — NATIVE DATA-DEPENDENT WRITE (scatter).
//
// This is the WRITE / scatter companion to 17-spmv's READ / gather
// (TASK-0341.03.01). It is the direct LHS analog of the gather: a
// data-dependent index, but in WRITE (left-hand-side) position rather
// than READ (right-hand-side) position.
//
// The sibling `prog.algo.nuc` fakes the per-input bin update with a
// rectangular masked accumulator: a `for i, for b` nest where
// `bin_inc(histogram[b], input[i], b)` returns `histogram[b]+1` iff
// `input[i] == b` else `histogram[b]`. That emulates the data-dependent
// address `histogram[input[i]]` with an O(N*BINS) dense scan + an
// equality mask in the kernel body. This variant expresses the address
// DIRECTLY and runs in O(N).
//
// What changed (vs prog.algo.nuc)
// -------------------------------
//   - The index of `histogram` on BOTH sides of the `<--` is itself a
//     runtime data read: `input[i]` is loaded from a `data` array and
//     used to index `histogram`. The v2 grammar already PARSES an index
//     as a full expression; the lowering pass (`lower_index_expr` with
//     `allow_gather = true`, set by `lower_indices` for the LHS and
//     `lower_data_ref` for the RHS) admits a data-dependent index in
//     array-subscript position (NOT in loop-bound position — a
//     data-dependent trip count is a separate, unimplemented feature).
//     The backend renders the LHS as a scatter store
//     `histogram[(input[<flat>]) as usize] = <rhs>;` (TASK-0376).
//   - There is NO `for b` and NO mask: a single `for i` walks the input
//     and updates exactly the one bin `input[i]` names. The compute
//     kernel is `inc(acc) = acc + 1` — a plain increment, no value/bin
//     arguments, because the address itself selects the bin.
//
// Pre-condition reused from prog.algo.nuc: every `input[i]` is already
// constrained to the bin range `[0, BINS)` (see the README + the
// committed `input.bin` is pre-clipped). So `input[i]` IS a valid bin
// index directly; no bucketing / clamping kernel is needed. A
// real-world histogram over UNCONSTRAINED input would still need a
// value->bin bucketing step, which today lives in a kernel (PRD §6.2.2)
// — that part is unchanged by this variant.
//
// Same-symbol read-modify-write. `histogram[input[i]]` is read (inside
// `inc(...)`) and written, to the SAME symbol, at the SAME
// data-dependent index. Single-assignment (PRD §6.2.1) is on the SYMBOL
// `histogram`, not on individual indices, so a repeated indexed write in
// a loop does not violate it. The codegen classifies this as an
// ACCUMULATE fan-in (NOT a cumulative cross-iteration array): the
// self-read index is structurally IDENTICAL to the LHS index, so the
// cumulative-array discriminator (which keys on a SHIFTED self-read
// index, as in jacobi/game-of-life) does not fire. `histogram` is
// pre-initialised to zero (the additive identity) by the codegen.
//
// Bit-identical to prog.algo.nuc's masked form and to reference.bin:
// the masked nest incremented `histogram[b]` once per `i` with
// `input[i] == b`, i.e. exactly once per `i` at `b == input[i]`, which
// is precisely what `histogram[input[i]] <-- inc(histogram[input[i]])`
// does. Same input.bin, same reference.bin, same N/BINS.
//
// Two schedules drive this algorithm: `scatter.sched.nuc`
// (single-worker, TASK-0376) and `distributed.scatter.sched.nuc`
// (TASK-0384), which partitions the input `i` loop across four compute
// workers — each scatters its disjoint input slice into its OWN PRIVATE
// full-width partial histogram (whole-array replicate, because the
// data-dependent write index makes `histogram`'s dim opaque), and the
// host element-wise-sums the partials (the TASK-0343 overlapping-write
// accumulator combine). The distributed scatter is SOUND because the
// partition is over the INPUT INDEX `i`, never over the histogram bins;
// a bin partition would be unsound and the compiler keeps it fail-loud
// (halo `is_scatter_rmw` gate). The distributed READ gather has the
// analogous "broaden" landing; see 17-spmv / TASK-0373.

const N    : usize = 256;     // total input length
const BINS : usize = 16;      // histogram bin count

// Input array and the BINS-wide accumulator output. Each `data` symbol
// is assigned by exactly one dataflow statement (PRD §6.2.1).
data input     : i32[N];
data histogram : i32[BINS];

// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated. Same
// signatures as prog.algo.nuc (so the load/save layout is unchanged).
kernel load_input  : ()          -> i32[N]    effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[BINS]) -> ()        effectful;

// Per-hit increment kernel — pure, reorderable. `inc(acc) = acc + 1`.
// No value/bin arguments: the data-dependent LHS address selects the
// bin directly, so there is nothing to mask. `wrapping_add` documents
// the overflow contract (PRD §10.1); the committed fixture's max bin
// count is far below i32::MAX.
// `combine=sum`: a distributed schedule that replicates `histogram`
// and partitions over `i` sums per-worker partials at the host
// (TASK-0343.01.01); maps to `wrapping_add` (byte-identical to the
// pre-attribute combine).
kernel inc         : (i32)        -> i32       pure combine=sum;

// Load the entire input on whoever owns I/O (the schedule decides).
input <-- load_input();

// Native scatter. `histogram` is pre-initialised to zero (additive
// identity). For each input element, increment the bin it names. The
// address `histogram[input[i]]` is a data-dependent WRITE; the read of
// the same slot inside `inc(...)` is the accumulate fan-in.
for i : 0 .. N {
    histogram[input[i]] <-- inc(histogram[input[i]]);
}

save_output(histogram);
